Done. Added to `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:18-57`, mirroring the Rust reference `store.rs` exactly.

**What was implemented**
- `public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable { case malformedFormat }`
- `public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note` — splits on the literal `\n---\n` separator into exactly 4 parts (`id`, `title`, `pinned` as `"1"`/`"0"`, `body`), builds a `Note`, and reuses the existing `extractTags(from:)` for tags.
- A small private `splitn` helper replicates Rust's `splitn(4, sep)` semantics: at most 3 splits, empty slices preserved.

**Why the helper** — `String.split` in this toolchain doesn't expose `omittingEmptySlices` combined with a fixed split count, so I matched the reference `store.rs:25` (`splitn(4, SEP)`) directly. This preserves a body that itself contains the separator and detects a wrong part count instead of silently truncating.

**Verified** (compiled a standalone copy of the logic and ran it):
- Valid note parses correctly (`id`, `title`, `pinned=true`, `tags=["world","swift"]`, `body`).
- 3 parts, 2 parts, empty string, and id-only all throw `NoteParseError.malformedFormat`.
- A body containing the separator is preserved as a single body field.
- Restored the fixture file to its clean form; it compiles cleanly as a library (`swiftc -parse-as-library -c`).

